     client-side JS, so anyone reading the page source can match a list of suspected addresses against the digests. It's
     meaningfully better than sending raw usernames, but if you're under GDPR you'd still treat the digest as personal
     data. If that undercuts the reason you picked hashing, the choice is worth revisiting now rather than after it
     ships.
  2. [1m"Production" is currently a hostname guess.[0m The repo has no deployment pipeline and no notion of environments, so
     the spec resolves environment from [38;5;153mdata-env[39m if set and falls back to inferring from the hostname. It's recorded as
     an explicit assumption to validate with whoever owns deploys — if a pipeline exists that can template the
     attribute, that replaces the heuristic.

  The spec also lists five prerequisites that are outside this repo and block going live: a Sentry project and DSN,
  vendor-side rate limits and allowed domains, the SRI hash for the pinned bundle, the application salt, and the
  deploy-environment confirmation.

  Please review it and let me know if you want changes before I write the implementation plan.

